// GESTURE-CAST fire. Off hand only, wrist trio only. Each alias is hardcoded to
//
// PLACEHOLDER BINDS -- bind to a throwaway test key, NOT the real grip/trigger/
// pad. Wiring these onto the actual controller buttons needs engine-side context
// gating that does not exist yet; without it a raw trigger read ALSO fires
// whatever the off hand's held weapon does on that same physical pull.



// ---------------------------------------------------------------- wheel
// From RS_WeaponWheel/keyconf.txt, which declared TWO sections of its own --
// "RSVR HUD" (wr_Keys) and "RSVR HUD - gun placement" (wr_PlaceKeys). Both are
// gone: this package has one section, for the same reason the header of this
// file gives. Nothing else had to change, because MENUDEF's rows bind the
// ALIAS name ("wr_toggle_off", "wr_place", ...) and never the section, so the
// wheel's own Control rows in MENUDEF.txt still resolve.
//
// NO NAME COLLISIONS with anything above: every alias here is wr_*, every
// alias above is rs_*, and the netevents they fire are wr_* against rs-*.

// -- the ring itself.
//
// THE ONLY DEFAULT BINDS IN THIS PACKAGE, and they are inherited rather than
// chosen -- everything above is deliberately unbound. They are kept because
// dropping them would silently change the wheel's out-of-box behaviour during
// what is meant to be a packaging change.
//
// THEY COLLIDE, and it is worth knowing exactly how. UZDXREMA's own
// engine/commonbinds.txt binds '[' to invprev and ']' to invnext, so on a
// desktop profile these two lines do nothing at all: FKeyBindings::DefaultBind
// refuses a key that is already bound and prints "Key already bound" rather
// than overwriting it. On the VR profiles (engine/vr/commonbinds.txt,
// vr/defbind0.txt, vr/defbind3.txt) inventory prev/next live on pgup/pgdn,
// mwheel and the dpad, the brackets are free, and these DO take. So the wheel
// opens on [ and ] in VR and appears unbound on a desktop config -- which is
// the right way round for a VR mod, but is not obvious from the file.
alias wr_toggle_off  "netevent wr_toggle_off"
alias wr_toggle_main "netevent wr_toggle_main"
addmenukey "Wheel: off hand"  wr_toggle_off
addmenukey "Wheel: main hand" wr_toggle_main
defaultbind ] wr_toggle_off
defaultbind [ wr_toggle_main

// Legacy spelling, kept so an already-written config that binds wr_toggle
// still works. It fires wr_toggle_off, exactly as it did in the source file --
// which makes it a synonym for the off-hand bind and NOT a general toggle,
// despite the name. Bind one or the other, not both.
alias wr_toggle      "netevent wr_toggle_off"

// -- ModelSwapper model cycle. Ours, not a compat guess, so it earns a real
// key rather than a gesture. No default bind: a model swap mid-fight from a
// fat-fingered key would be worse than not having the feature.
alias wr_ms_cycle_main "netevent wr_ms_cycle_main"
alias wr_ms_cycle_off  "netevent wr_ms_cycle_off"
addmenukey "Wheel: cycle main-hand model" wr_ms_cycle_main
addmenukey "Wheel: cycle off-hand model"  wr_ms_cycle_off

// -- gun readout placement. A calibration tool for whoever is measuring a
// weapon set, not a normal-play control, so no default binds -- none of these
// can fire by accident during a fight because none is on a key until you put
// it there.
//
// Bindable FROM THE MENU, not console-only: this is used in VR, where there is
// no reachable console, so an alias with no menu row is not "advanced", it is
// unreachable. The four verbs below are the whole workflow. The sixteen nudge
// aliases stay config-only -- eight axes times two directions would bury the
// key list, and anyone binding all sixteen is editing a config file anyway.
alias wr_place   "netevent wr_place"
alias wr_lock    "netevent wr_lock"
alias wr_measure "netevent wr_measure"
alias wr_save    "netevent wr_save"
addmenukey "Wheel: enter/exit placement mode" wr_place
addmenukey "Wheel: lock touch point"          wr_lock
addmenukey "Wheel: two-point size measure"    wr_measure
addmenukey "Wheel: print calibration line"    wr_save

